smoke (capture->act->confirm, Exception Approved)\",\r\n57:   \"hold your turn until every bgrun you start has its END/TIMEOUT line; LabVIEW gone before return\",\r\n58:   \"facts only; result/1 <=10 facts <=3 open\"\r\n59:  ],\r\n60:  \"escalation\": 2,\r\n61:  \"retry_of_card\": \"90-5\",\r\n62:  \"advances\": [\"M8\", \"M3\", \"R1\", \"R3\"]\r\n63: }\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$n=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'archive/peer/2026-09-26-c90-t0step3-movewire.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$n++; '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ }'","aggregated_output":"1: # c90-t0step3-movewire\r\n2: \r\n3: - **agent:** claude\r\n4: - **role:** hypothesis\r\n5: - **model:** claude-opus-5-5 (effort high; peer.ps1 default for role hypothesis)\r\n6: - **kind:** review\r\n7: - **cost:** $2.1434  in 36 / out 26673 / cache-create 137582 / cache-read 2219939  (309s, 26 turn(s))\r\n8: - **date:** 2026-09-26 04:47:15\r\n9: - **outcome:** ANSWERED (312s)\r\n10: - **verdict-card:** (no -ReviewCard)\r\n11: - **why asked:** (Claude fills in)\r\n12: - **verdict:** unverified\r\n13: \r\n14: ## Question\r\n15: \r\n16: ATTACK this claim (failed prediction, card 90-5, log tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.log, script tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.py, run 1).\r\n17: \r\n18: CONTEXT: step 3 of PD196(d) (docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1049-1105) instruments a byte copy of claudeDev\\D1_s1_copy.vi with\r\n19: t0stamp CLFN nodes (gscript.build_clfn, top level only) moved into nested diagrams by stagekit.move_in (OpMoveIn_v0 = GObject.Move\r\n20: by uid). Each CLFN needs param1 `site` (t6) = an I32 constant and param2 `any` (t8) = a BRANCH of an existing wire\r\n21: (OpConnectFromWire_v0).\r\n22: \r\n23: OBSERVED (diag_c90_t0_step3.log:24-30, 32-53, 196-199):\r\n24:  - S00: the constant was created on the CLFN's t6 while the CLFN was TOP LEVEL (gscript.create_const_loop_term 'for_n', uid #23090,\r\n25:    err ''), then move_in(CLFN #22968 -> Diagram[43]) and move_in(const #23090 -> Diagram[43]) both returned err ''. Afterwards the\r\n26:    CLFN's t6 wire reads 0 and the Tunnel/LoopTunnel/SelectorTunnel censuses are unchanged (468/132/146 before and after).\r\n27:  - S02..S20 (7 While-body sites): CLFN moved first, then OpCreateConstOnTerm_v0 (WhileLoop[i].Diagram.Nodes[n].Terms[6]) created\r\n28:    the constant in place, t6 wire non-zero. connect_from_wire(sink t8 <- Wire(site wire).Terms[source]) returned err '' and\r\n29:    Is Broken? False but Wire count delta 0 (my gate expected +1).\r\n30:  - E1 ExecState 0 at the end; computation_diff(S1, new) rows 0, removed 0, added 25 = CallLibrary + DigitalNumericConstant + 9 Invoke.\r\n31: \r\n32: MY EXPLANATION (the claim to attack):\r\n33:  1. GObject.Move of the two ends one at a time DROPS the wire between a top-level constant and a node once the node crosses into a\r\n34:     nested diagram (no tunnel is auto-created by scripting, unlike a GUI drag); so a typed constant must be created IN PLACE with\r\n35:     Terminal.Create Constant addressed through the owning Loop (WhileLoop or ForLoop [i].Diagram.Nodes[n]). A case FRAME cannot be\r\n36:     addressed this way (CaseStructure has Diagrams[], no Loop.Diagram), so sites 6/7/14/15 are skipped in run 2.\r\n37:  2. Wire delta 0 on connect_from_wire is the expected BRANCH outcome (skill law: a successful branch adds no Wire object); the right\r\n38:     gate is the sink terminal's wire uid == the site wire (read by node_terms).\r\n39:  3. ExecState 0 came from the 9 leftover junk `Invoke` nodes (unwired `reference` - docs/NAMES.md:307-313) minted by build_clfn /\r\n40:     the creators, which run 1 purged only after connect_from_wire; run 2 purges after every creator.\r\n41: \r\n42: ALREADY RULED OUT: the moves themselves erroring (err '' on both, node found on Diagram[43] uid echo); tunnels being created (censuses\r\n43: equal); OpCreateConstOnTerm_v0 failing on While bodies (7/7 created, t6 wired).\r\n44: \r\n45: Give: the strongest reason any of 1-3 is wrong; an alternative explanation for t6 wire 0 after the moves and for ExecState 0; what\r\n46: would falsify each; the cheapest discriminating test. Also: is there ANY scripting route (VI Server method on Terminal / Diagram /\r\n47: CaseStructure frame) to put a typed, wired constant on a node inside a case frame with the ops this project owns\r\n48: (docs/toolkit-capabilities.md rows 69-71)?\r\n49: \r\n50: \r\n51: --- HOW TO ANSWER (mandatory, from the dispatcher) ---\r\n52: Your job is to REFUTE the claim above, not to confirm it. Do not open with agreement.\r\n53: 1. Name the single strongest reason the claim is WRONG.\r\n54: 2. Name at least one ALTERNATIVE explanation of the same evidence.\r\n55: 3. Name the observation that would FALSIFY the claim.\r\n56: 4. End with the CHEAPEST discriminating test that separates the claim from your alternative.\r\n57: If you still believe the claim holds after all four, say so explicitly and state what would change your mind.\r\n58: \r\n59: ## Answer\r\n60: \r\n61: **Verdict: parts of all three claims are wrong. Claim 2 holds, but for evidence the claim never cited. Claim 3 names the wrong source for the junk and never measured the cause. Claim 1 was inferred without any measurement, and its main conclusion, that case frames are unreachable, is false.**\r\n62: \r\n63: ## 1. Strongest reason the claim is wrong: claim 3 blames the wrong op\r\n64: \r\n65: The 9 leftover `Invoke` nodes come from `move_in`, one per call. Neither `build_clfn` nor the constant creators left them:\r\n66: \r\n67: - **`build_clfn` cleans up after itself.** It deletes its own junk Invoke (`tools/gscript.py:3002-3005`).\r\n68: - **Constants are not in the node census.** The census counts `report_all('Node')` (`tools/recipes/build_d1_m3a1.py:487-492`). The main VI's census by class has no numeric-constant class (`docs/NAMES.md:341-344`). So a new constant adds 0 rows.\r\n69: - **The arithmetic matches exactly if each move adds one node:**\r\n70:   - Site 0: 625 before move 1 → 626 before move 2 (`diag_c90_t0_step3.log:26,28`). That is +1 with nothing but a move in between.\r\n71:   - Site 0's second move, plus site 2's CLFN build: 626 → 628 at `:33`.\r\n72:   - Site 2's move plus its constant: 628 → 629 at `:33→37`. Move +1, constant 0.\r\n73:   - Site 8: 629 after purge → 630 at `:60` (CLFN build only).\r\n74:   - The scratch run shows it too: 625 → 627 across one move plus one build (`diag_c90_t0_place.log:28,34`).\r\n75:   - Nine `move_in` calls (2 at site 0, 1 at each of 7 sites) = 9 Invokes, which is exactly `added 25 = 8 CLFN + 8 constants + 9 Invoke` (`:198`).\r\n76: - **This was seen before.** `docs/cycle27-plan.md:2562` records \"the same junk `Invoke` cycle 62's `move_in` purged (#9317)\".\r\n77: \r\n78: Run 2's purges will probably catch these anyway, but by accident. The \"after const\" purge compares against the census taken *before* the move (`tools/stagekit.py:552-568`). That makes it only as reliable as the claim that no constant is ever counted as a node.\r\n79: \r\n80: The cause is also unmeasured. The ExecState timeline has only two readings: 1 after open, 0 after all sites (`t0_sites_s1_step3.json:10-31`). Every per-connect reading was 0, and each was taken while that connect's own junk still existed. Nobody ever read ExecState with zero junk present.\r\n81: \r\n82: ## 2. Alternative explanations\r\n83: \r\n84: **For ExecState 0 (other things present when E1 was read):**\r\n85: - **(a)** Site 0's CLFN is still in Diagram[43] with t6 **and** t8 unwired. Its constant is left orphaned.\r\n86: - **(b)** A type the Call Library node refuses to adapt. t8 is \"Adapt to Type\" with the Constant flag, passed by value (`diag_c90_t0_step3.py:54-55`). NI documents that Adapt to Type breaks the node for some data types, e.g. an array of strings inside a cluster ([NI forum](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Call-Library-Function-Adapt-Interface-to-Data-Breaking/td-p/3832312), [NI KB](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019L0sSAE)). The display-body wires (w19372/19465/19468/19429) could carry image references or clusters. `Is Broken? False` describes the *wire*, not whether the node accepts the type.\r\n87: \r\n88: **For t6 reading 0 after the site-0 moves:**\r\n89: - Nothing ever showed that t6 was wired *before* the moves. Gate S00.2 checks only the returned uid and err (`:25`).\r\n90: - `OpCreateConstTop_v0` has been measured only on a For loop's N terminal (`docs/NAMES.md:320-323`). \"Any top-level node terminal works too\" is a docstring claim (`gscript.py:2640`).\r\n91: - So \"the constant was never wired to a CLFN parameter\" has not been ruled out.\r\n92: - Even if it was wired, the run cannot tell which of the two moves dropped the wire.\r\n93: - The claim also says \"no tunnel is auto-created by scripting\". That is true only of `Move`. `Terminal.Connect Wire` across a boundary *does* create tunnels: the NI forum says \"the tunnel will automatically be connected for you\" ([NI forum](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Programmatically-creating-a-tunnel-with-LV-scripting/td-p/1830745)), and it was measured here as `LoopTunnel 0 → 2` (`docs/toolkit-capabilities.md:73`).\r\n94: - The LabVIEW Wiki page for `GObject.Move` says nothing about wires ([labviewwiki](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/GObject_class/Move_method)).\r\n95: \r\n96: ## 3. What would falsify each claim\r\n97: \r\n98: - **Claim 1:** reading the top-level t6 wire right after `create_const_loop_term` gives 0. The fault is then the creator, not `Move`. Or: case-frame sites succeed through the route in section 5.\r\n99: - **Claim 2:** already settled in its favour. The connect op's own readback is `UID 2` = the sink terminal's wire, and it equals the site wire at all 7 sites (5859, 541, 19372, 19465, 19468, 19429, 34066; `t0_sites_s1_step3.json:66,91,116,141,166,191,216`). The branch landed. Cite this, not \"delta 0 is expected\".\r\n100: - **Claim 3:** after deleting every new Invoke that has no wired terminal, ExecState is still 0.\r\n101: \r\n102: ## 4. Cheapest discriminating test\r\n103: \r\n104: One scratch run with no rebuild and about 8 LabVIEW calls:\r\n105: \r\n106: 1. **Site 0 only:**\r\n107:    - `create_const_loop_term` → read t6 at top level with `node_terms(W, 0, n)`.\r\n108:    - Move the CLFN → read t6.\r\n109:    - Move the constant → read t6.\r\n110:    - Take a node census around each move.\r\n111: \r\n112:    This settles when (or whether) the wire existed and dropped, and confirms that each `move_in` adds 1 Invoke.\r\n113: 2. **Replay run 1's 7 While-body sites, then bisect ExecState:**\r\n114:    - Delete the new zero-wired Invokes (expect 9) → read ExecState.\r\n115:    - If it is 0: delete site 0's CLFN → read ExecState.\r\n116:    - If still 0: delete one site CLFN at a time → read ExecState after each. The site that turns it to 1 names the refused Adapt-to-Type type.\r\n117: \r\n118: ## 5. Case frames are reachable with ops the project already owns\r\n119: \r\n120: \"A case frame cannot be addressed\" is only true of `OpCreateConstOnTerm_v0`'s `Loop.Diagram` route. The `Traverse('Diagram')[i]` route addresses frames. This run's own `find_node`, `node_terms` and `connect_from_wire` all index by Diagram number, and the frame sites 76, 75 and 132 are in that same index space.\r\n121: \r\n122: Route with existing ops:\r\n123: 1. `OpCreateConstTop_v0` on the top-level CLFN's t6. The constant comes out typed I32 with its value set.\r\n124: 2. `move_in` the CLFN and then the constant into Diagram[frame]. The wire drops, as run 1 showed, and the junk from each move is purged.\r\n125: 3. `OpConnectNested_v0` (or `_v1` with both indices set to the same diagram) on Diagram[frame]: sink = CLFN t6, source = constant t0.\r\n126: 4. Read the value back with `OpConstValueN_v1`.\r\n127: \r\n128: No tunnel is created, so rule 1a is safe. One caveat: `_v0` is verified for wire identity but not for ExecState (`toolkit-capabilities.md:72`).\r\n129: \r\n130: `OpCreateConst_v0` is **not** a usable route: it produces an empty-valued `Constant` (`toolkit-capabilities.md:69-70`).\r\n131: \r\n132: A cleaner alternative is a new op, which the standing tool permission allows: `OpCreateConstOnTerm_v0` with its ladder head swapped for `OpConnectNested_v0`'s `Traverse('Diagram')[i] → To More Specific Class → Nodes[]` sink ladder.\r\n133: \r\n134: ## Side hazard: purged uids are reused\r\n135: \r\n136: After every purge, the next new CLFN gets the uid of the Invoke just deleted, 6 times in a row:\r\n137: - #23402 at `:40` → `:59`\r\n138: - #23477 at `:67` → `:81`\r\n139: - #23898 at `:89` → `:103`\r\n140: - #23910 at `:111` → `:125`\r\n141: - #24309 at `:133` → `:147`\r\n142: - #24513 at `:155` → `:173`\r\n143: \r\n144: Any cached uid set, such as `new_since` or the \"still present\" checks, can alias across a purge.\r\n145: \r\n146: ## Sources\r\n147: \r\n148: (extract from answer)\r\n149: \r\n150: ## What was done with it\r\n151: \r\n152: Read by the card 90-5 material session (fable/low) at 04:48, after its 70-min budget was nearly spent; NO second LabVIEW\r\n153: run was launched on it. Accepted as facts, applied to `tools/bench/diag_c90_t0_step3.py` before any rerun:\r\n154: - §1 the 9 junk Invokes are `move_in`'s (one per call, census arithmetic `diag_c90_t0_step3.log:26-37`), not `build_clfn`'s\r\n155:   — the script's docstring said \"build_clfn / the creators\"; run 2's `junk_purge` after every `_op` still deletes them (the\r\n156:   mark is taken before each move), so the code stands and only the attribution was wrong.\r\n157: - §3 claim 2 is settled by the op's own `UID 2` readback (= the site wire at all 7 sites) — run 2's gate reads t8 through\r\n158:   `node_terms` (the same fact by another reader); cite `UID 2` too.\r\n159: - §2(a)/(b) + §4 step 2 (bisect ExecState 0: purge the zero-wired Invokes, then delete site 0's CLFN, then one CLFN at a\r\n160:   time — an Adapt-to-Type refusal on a display-body wire is a live alternative) — NOT run; it is the next cheapest test.\r\n161: - §5 case frames ARE reachable (`OpCreateConstTop_v0` at top level → move both → `OpConnectNested_v0/_v1` on Diagram[frame]\r\n162:   const t0 → CLFN t6, no tunnel) — a design choice for the judgement session (run 2 as written SKIPS sites 6/7/14/15).\r\n163: - Side hazard (purged uids reused by the next CLFN, 6 times) noted; run 2 addresses nodes by uid echo after each find_node.\r\n164: Left for judgement: whether to rerun the patched script (13 sites) or first add the §5 route (17 sites); whether the §4\r\n165: bisect runs before either. Rule 1a is unaffected (stamps read time only).\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
